          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-tenant-of-wildfell-hall-49290568c97acfb4\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Arthur presents his love as an already established emotional fact—something Helen supposedly knows—and uses that avowal to reassure her and imply that she should be satisfied. The utterance makes no distinct commitment or relational undertaking, and nothing indicates that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Arthur's sincerity and the adequacy or quality of his love are open to doubt, but those contextual questions do not alter the utterance's strongly truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Arthur's original direct address to Helen is represented as quoted dialogue within Helen's diary narrative.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Arthur responds to Helen's tears with a tender gesture and an avowal framed as familiar knowledge. His follow-up—“what more could you desire?”—makes the avowal self-satisfied and minimizing: he treats professed love as sufficient despite the neglect and excesses that trouble Helen. The surrounding passage therefore complicates the value and sincerity of his love, while the core words remain an assertion or avowal rather than an undertaking or expressive outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“you know that I love you”\",\"supports\":\"The complement of “know” presents Arthur's love as an existing fact shared or already recognized by Helen, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Arthur asks, “why do you cry?” and follows the avowal with “and what more could you desire?”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance functions as reassurance and as a minimizing answer to Helen's distress, but does not itself undertake a commitment; this supports high T and low P.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He presses Helen's hand to his “feverish lips” while speaking.\",\"supports\":\"The gesture marks tenderness and emotional delivery, but supplies no positive evidence that the words themselves are an involuntary or reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Helen has been caring for Arthur after his prolonged absence and worries about his conduct; she answers that she wants him to love himself as truly and faithfully as she loves him.\",\"supports\":\"The context exposes a mismatch between Arthur's avowal and Helen's deeper ethical concern, explaining the utterance's potentially self-serving reassurance without requiring P, E, or O.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Arthur's tenderness, reassurance, and self-serving minimization are contextual features rather than independent performative, expressive/reflexive, or residual core forces.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
